[38;5;231m⏺[39m Time-based only. That's a lean system: no event store, no cross-tab plumbing, no recurrence engine.

  One hard constraint to put on the table before I ask about delivery, because it's the kind of thing that's miserable
  to discover late: [1ma serverless browser app cannot reliably notify you when the tab is closed.[0m Web push requires a
  server to send it, and the browser APIs for locally-scheduled notifications aren't dependable across browsers. So a
  reminder can only reach you while the app is actually open. If "ping me at 9am whether or not I have the app open" is
  the real point of this feature, then the serverless decision needs revisiting now rather than after we build it.

  Within "while the app is open," the fork is how loud it gets.

  - [1mIn-app only[0m — a badge and a panel listing what's due or overdue, rendered by the app itself. No permission prompt,
    no browser APIs, works identically everywhere, fully testable. Can't reach you if you're on another tab.
  - [1mPlus OS-level toasts[0m — the browser [38;5;153mNotification[39m API surfaces a real desktop notification, so it reaches you on
    another tab in the same browser session. Costs a permission prompt, needs a denied/blocked fallback path, and is
    awkward to test. Cheap to add on top of in-app later; the in-app layer is the thing that has to exist either way.

  [1mMy recommendation:[0m in-app as the source of truth, with OS toasts as an optional escalation layered over it. Building
  toasts first would mean a feature that silently does nothing for anyone who clicks "Block."

